# The trust root for rollout evidence. PUBLIC KEYS ONLY.
#
# One line per ENVIRONMENT PRINCIPAL, in OpenSSH allowed_signers format
# (`ssh-keygen -Y verify -f <this file>`, and `git verify-tag` under
# gpg.format=ssh). backend/scripts/lib/rollout-signing.ts is the only reader.
#
# WHY ONE KEY PER ENVIRONMENT, NOT ONE PER PERSON. The question a receipt has to
# answer is "which environment agent ran this step", because that is what
# rollout-procedure.md's host split turns on: the stage host cannot run the
# cutover, and a receipt claiming it did is the failure mode worth catching. A
# distinct key per environment means a stage key CANNOT sign a production step —
# that separation is the file's whole content, and it is why these are never one
# shared key with two names.
#
# NEVER a private key. `id_ed25519` (no `.pub`) or a line containing
# "PRIVATE KEY" is a leak, and scripts/tests/unit/rollout-receipt-redaction.test.ts
# reads this exact file on every PR to say so.
#
# BOOTSTRAP ENTRIES. The two keys below were generated when this file was
# created, so the allow-list has a well-formed, distinct entry per environment
# from its first commit — their private halves were never written down and no
# agent holds them, so nothing can sign as either principal yet. That is the
# intended state: provisioning the real host keys is deliberately a separate
# change (see the "Receipts" subsection of docs/runbooks/rollout-procedure.md,
# which documents the host-side setup). Registering a real key means replacing
# that principal's line with the contents of the host's own `.pub` file, in a
# reviewed commit — the review IS the key-distribution ceremony.

